description:
Per wyrd-f295 Phase 2 + the dead-rando audit (2026-05-12) that found ~52% of dead-rando entries are scribal-variants or inflections that should dedupe.

Add 'data/mining/curation_lemma_overrides.jsonl' — cross-source curation event log. Row shape:

  {"_op":"patch","_type":"etymon","ref":"oe:caelf","lemma_ref":"oe:cealf"}
  {"_op":"patch","_type":"etymon","ref":"on:vath","merged_into_ref":"on:vað"}

Curation = append events. Used when auto-clustering produces wrong assignments. Replay applies overrides AFTER auto-clustering.

Depends on: Phase 2a

Removed from welsh_place_names.json on 2026-05-08 as a workaround for wyrd-p8ve (canonical_decompositions exponential blow-up on inputs > ~40 chars). The 58-character Welsh village is the famous longest place name in Europe and SHOULD be in the corpus — restore it once canonical_decompositions has been rewritten with score-pruning during the walk (not after).

Also worth running a length-distribution scan on every culture's place_names corpus to identify any other near-pathological inputs that work today but flirt with the explosion threshold:

    .venv/bin/python -c "
    import json
    for c in ['english','scottish','welsh','irish','breton']:
        d = json.load(open(f'wyrd/generators/kenning/data/{c}_place_names.json'))
        # walk nested dict -> list of names, find max-length entries
        names = []
        def walk(n):
            if isinstance(n, str): names.append(n)
            elif isinstance(n, dict):
                for v in n.values(): walk(v)
            elif isinstance(n, list):
                for v in n: walk(v)
        walk(d)
        names.sort(key=len, reverse=True)
        print(f'{c} max len: {len(names[0])} ({names[0]!r}); top 5 lens: {[len(n) for n in names[:5]]}')
    "

